-- title:
--   The $\mathbb Z[i]$-lattice $\Lambda$ generated by a set of vectors
-- statement:
--   For a finite list $\mathcal W$ of vectors of $W=\mathbb C^n$, $\Lambda(\mathcal W)$ is the $\mathbb Z[i]$-module generated by $\mathcal W$:
--   $$\Lambda(\mathcal W)=\Big\{\textstyle\sum_{w\in\mathcal W}c_w\,w\;:\;c_w\in\mathbb Z[i]\Big\}.$$
--   In §9.1, $\Lambda_i=\Lambda(\mathcal W_i)$ is the lattice generated by the vectors computed so far; these lattices increase along the interpolation phase, $\Lambda_0\subseteq\Lambda_1\subseteq\cdots\subseteq\Lambda_N$, and the relative indices $[\Lambda_i:\Lambda_{i-1}]$ record the divisions.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\Lambda(\mathcal W)$ is the additive subgroup of $\mathbb C^n$ generated by all products $c\,w$ with $c\in\mathbb Z[i]$ (through `GaussianInt.toComplex`) and $w\in\mathcal W$. Since this set of generators is closed under multiplication by $\mathbb Z[i]$, the subgroup is the $\mathbb Z[i]$-span; using an additive subgroup makes $[\Lambda_i:\Lambda_{i-1}]$ Mathlib's `AddSubgroup.relIndex`.
-- source:
--   Y. Spiizer, R. S. Steiner, Rational and Gaussian long integer multiplication, manuscript (STOC 2027 submission; held version sha256 f50b53628a53a03bd55785424af241d0ac5ea751714330838e4017b90b27ad70), §9.1, p. 30 (PDF p. 32)

import Mathlib

namespace GaussToomCook

/-- **The ℤ[i]-lattice generated by a set of vectors**, Spiizer, Steiner, *Rational and Gaussian long integer multiplication*, manuscript (STOC 2027 submission; sha256 f50b5362…27ad70), §9.1, p. 30
(PDF 32): "we shall consider the ℤ[i]-modules Λ_i ⊆ W generated by W_i".

`lattice W` is the ℤ[i]-span of the vectors in the list `W` inside `W = Fin n → ℂ`.

**Formalization Note.** It is defined as the additive subgroup generated by all multiples `c • w` with
`c ∈ ℤ[i]` (acting through `GaussianInt.toComplex`) and `w ∈ W`. This set of generators is closed under
multiplication by `ℤ[i]`, so the additive subgroup it generates is exactly the ℤ[i]-span; it is used as an
additive subgroup so that relative indices `[Λ_i : Λ_{i−1}]` are `AddSubgroup.relIndex`, with no
`Module ℤ[i] ℂ` instance needed. -/
def lattice {n : ℕ} (W : List (Fin n → ℂ)) : AddSubgroup (Fin n → ℂ) :=
  AddSubgroup.closure {x | ∃ c : GaussianInt, ∃ w ∈ W, x = GaussianInt.toComplex c • w}

end GaussToomCook


